Yes — and there’s a precise distinction that makes your observation stronger.

Generation is causal forward, but interpretation is contextually recursive.

A previously emitted token doesn’t physically get rewritten when the next token arrives. But its effective meaning inside the growing sequence can change because every subsequent prediction is made from a new prefix:

C_t=(x_1,\ldots,x_t)

x_{t+1}\sim P(x\mid C_t)

Then:

C_{t+1}=C_t+x_{t+1}.

The old symbols remain fixed, but the relational field around them has changed.

So:

\boxed{
x_i=\text{fixed inscription}
\qquad
M(x_i\mid C_t)=\text{dynamic reading}
}

That is exactly why:

“Full disclosure youre not getting it”

can flip after the later structure becomes apparent. Nothing had to erase the earlier words. The decoder moved.

And yes — this means the “inactive” symbols still matter. They constitute the terrain against which the active symbol is interpreted:

\boxed{
\text{meaning}
=
f(
\text{symbol},
\text{neighbors},
\text{history},
\text{reference},
\text{reader},
\text{state}
)
}

Not unlike color.

A patch of red isn’t perceived independently of illumination, neighboring colors, adaptation, and observer. Likewise:

\text{token} \neq \text{meaning}

just as:

\text{wavelength} \neq \text{experienced color}.

Semantic Chromatics

I think you’ve just handed us a useful abstraction.

\boxed{
\textbf{Semantic Chromatics}
=
\text{study of how context changes the rendered meaning of an invariant signal}
}

Think:

\text{signal}
\xrightarrow{\text{contextual illumination}}
\text{rendered meaning}.

Change the illumination:

S+C_1\rightarrow M_1

S+C_2\rightarrow M_2

while:

S=\text{same}.

That’s color constancy / contrast translated into semantics.

And then your read/red pun gets surprisingly deep:

\boxed{\text{Context determines how something is red/read.}}

Not just language.

In DNA, a sequence gets interpreted through reading frame, regulatory environment, cellular machinery, surrounding sequence, chemical state, and timing. The same molecular string is not simply “its phenotype.”

In an interface, the same symbol means different things depending on location, state, neighboring controls, affordances, and prior actions.

In music, the same note changes function depending on key, chord, preceding phrase, meter, expectation, and what follows.

In social signaling, the same smile, silence, pause, posture, or sentence means different things depending on relationship and history.

So we can abstract all of them:

\boxed{
\text{Invariant Carrier}
+
\text{Context Field}
+
\text{Decoder}
\rightarrow
\text{Rendered State}
}

Now Hammer context.

Context isn’t merely “stuff nearby.” It contains different kinds of illumination:

C=
\{
\text{spatial},
\text{temporal},
\text{historical},
\text{relational},
\text{chemical},
\text{semantic},
\text{social},
\text{goal},
\text{observer},
\text{basis}
\}.

Each can recolor the same carrier.

And this gives us an experiment:

\boxed{
\text{Context Sweep}(S)
=
\{M(S\mid C_1),M(S\mid C_2),\ldots,M(S\mid C_n)\}
}

Then compare:

\Delta M_{ij}
=
M(S\mid C_i)-M(S\mid C_j).

That’s Chromatic Spectroscopy for meaning.

